Lab 5: Deep Learning Model Security in the Cloud
Objective
Simulate secure cloud model storage by mimicking cloud bucket behavior locally, implementing encryption, and practicing access control strategies — all without real cloud access.

Prerequisites
Start your Al Nafi-provided Ubuntu machine

Ensure the following are installed:

gpg for file encryption

openssl (already preinstalled on most Ubuntu systems)

Basic knowledge of directory and file permissions

You will simulate S3/GCP Storage using local folders and encrypted files.

Steps
Step 1: Simulate a Secure Cloud Storage Bucket
Create a local directory to simulate an S3 or GCP bucket:

mkdir -p ~/secure-cloud-storage/model-bucket
cd ~/secure-cloud-storage/model-bucket
Copy a sample model file (or create a mock one):

echo "This is a mock ML model." > model.pt
Set strict access permissions (simulating private bucket access):

chmod 600 model.pt
Add a simple access policy (simulated) in bucket-policy.json:

{
  "Version": "2025-07-09",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Principal": "*",
      "Action": "GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::model-bucket/model.pt",
      "Condition": {
        "Bool": {
          "aws:SecureTransport": "false"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
Step 2: Encrypt the Model File
Option A: Using GPG (preferred for symmetric encryption)
Encrypt the file:

gpg -c model.pt
You’ll be prompted to enter a passphrase. This creates model.pt.gpg.

Remove the original unencrypted file:

shred -u model.pt
To decrypt later:

gpg model.pt.gpg
Option B: Using OpenSSL (alternative method)
openssl aes-256-cbc -salt -in model.pt -out model.pt.enc
shred -u model.pt
openssl aes-256-cbc -d -in model.pt.enc -out model.pt
Step 3: Simulate Access Logging and Controls
Log every "download" attempt:

echo "$(date): model accessed by user" >> access.log
View the access logs:

cat access.log
Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Simulated secure cloud storage using a local folder as a private bucket

Used Linux permissions to restrict access to the model file

Applied GPG/OpenSSL to encrypt and protect the file

Wrote a mock access policy and audit log system

These steps help you understand how to secure deep learning models in real cloud storage (like S3 or GCP Storage) using encryption, access control, and logging — without needing live cloud access.
